 Keep the pyramid fixed and press SEED at the desk. Two adjacent cells now begin the history. Build a few rows again. Where do the familiar triangular relations return, and do they ever overlap? Press SEED once more to return to the single cell. How much of the resemblance belonged to the rule, and how much to the beginning we gave it?
 
+Before leaving the desk, turn RULE until the plate reads 222 and step from the single seed. The triangle fills solid, reaches round the ring, and stops changing. Turn it to 190: stripes, every cell repeating the same short cycle. Press 30: no row can be read off the one before it by eye. Press 110: structures that persist and travel, meeting and breaking, with no way to say from the picture when the next will appear. Stephen Wolfram sorted the 256 rules into four such classes in 1984 — uniform, repeating, random, complex — and set the last apart as the one that behaves like the things around us.[^ca-classes] The plate captions 110 “Turing Complete”; carry that word to the hall called Edge of Chaos, ahead, where the border between the third class and the fourth is the whole question.
