 We will return to the pyramid's way of making in Fractals. First, two almost matching volumes will let us follow one changed value through the same procedure. We have asked how similar appearances can be made differently. What happens when we keep the making the same and alter one small part of the beginning?
 
-[^ca-resemblance]: An organised appearance alone does not demonstrate an agent, a circuit or a general-purpose computer. Such claims require an account of states, operations and the results they can produce. This comparison establishes a question about resemblance and procedure, not a proof of computational universality.
+[^ca-resemblance]: The explorer's plate captions Rule 90 “Sierpiński” and Rule 110 “Turing Complete”. This comparison earns the first word only as a question about resemblance. The second is a theorem rather than a caption: Matthew Cook proved in 2004 that Rule 110 can carry out any computation (“Universality in elementary cellular automata”, *Complex Systems* 15), by reading its persisting structures as signals and their collisions as logic — an account of states, operations and results that no organised appearance supplies on its own. The Rule 110 display in the first hall is where to begin asking for it.
+
+[^ca-classes]: Stephen Wolfram, “Universality and complexity in cellular automata”, *Physica D* 10 (1984).
